# Power-failure handling on a node. Three files in /etc/furcate, all root-owned.
# Nothing is stopped or powered off until the operator turns it on, and a
# node with none of these files does nothing about the supply.
#
# 1. ups.json - where to ask about the supply. Read only: the node asks the
#    NUT daemon (upsd) over the overlay with LIST VAR and sends nothing else.
#    A credential, if upsd ever needs one, is a systemd credential named here,
#    never a file in the repository or a command-line argument.
#
#   {
#     "source": { "host": "192.0.2.5", "port": 3493, "ups": "mainsupply" },
#     "triggers": {
#       "dwell_secs": 20,
#       "confirm_reads": 3, "confirm_secs": 20, "commbad_secs": 60,
#       "capture_charge_pct": 80, "capture_after_secs": 180,
#       "quiesce_charge_pct": 65, "quiesce_after_secs": 420,
#       "halt_charge_pct": 50,    "halt_after_secs": 900,
#       "trust_lb": false
#     }
#   }
#
#   An outage is acted on only after confirm_reads consecutive on-battery
#   reads spread over confirm_secs. Losing upsd while on line is logged as
#   COMMBAD and nothing more.
#
# 2. ups-shutdown - this node's authority and turn (key=value).
#
#   mode=dry-run            # dry-run (default) or enforce
#   delay_secs=0            # seconds after the ladder reaches halt before this
#                           # node starts stopping itself; dependents small,
#                           # the node that holds the supply's USB cable last
#   ups_host=no             # yes on that one node; needs delay_secs >= 120
#
#   To go live: set mode=enforce here. To stop everything on one node at once:
#     sudo touch /etc/furcate/ups-actuation-disabled      (remove it to re-enable)
#
# 3. ups-stop - what to stop on the way down, one unit per line. Stop order is
#    the reverse of bring-up: a unit is stopped before the units it needs.
#    systemd sends SIGTERM and waits; the ladder kills nothing. timeout is the
#    window for a stop; grace is a longer window for a job that checkpoints on
#    SIGTERM. stop= names a service's own safe-stop executable (absolute path,
#    root-owned, run before the unit is stopped).
#
#   unit=batch-job.service      needs=database.service timeout=30 grace=300
#   unit=database.service       needs=secrets.service  timeout=120 stateful=yes
#   unit=secrets.service        timeout=60 stateful=yes
#   unit=chain-node.service     needs=secrets.service  timeout=120 stateful=yes stop=/usr/local/libexec/node-safe-stop
